         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nThis text presents an application for practicing division. Before using it, we recommend studying the previous texts in this chapter (<htcite0>), which introduce the multiplication tables and other basic ideas you will need here.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_practicing",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Practice\n\nReading a text is a good start, but truly understanding division takes practice. In this application, the **Multiplication Tables** menu lets you choose which table to work with. By default it is set to **Randomly**, so a new divisor is chosen each time; if you prefer to focus on a single table, select a number from 1 to 9.\n\nThe exercise uses the **long division algorithm** (<htcite0>), the step-by-step method for dividing numbers by hand. Remember: when one number does not divide another exactly, the amount that is left over is called the **remainder**.\n\nIn the following table, we propose dividing one number by another. Use the side arrows (<i class=\"fa-solid fa-caret-up upArrowWithFA\" style=\"font-size: 1.5em\" name=\"traineeUp\"></i> <i class=\"fa-solid fa-caret-down downArrowWithFA\" style=\"font-size: 1.5em\" name=\"traineeDown\"></i>) to check each part of the calculation, and try to work out the answer before looking at it.\n\nWhen you finish, you will receive a medal (<i class=\"fa-solid fa-medal\" style=\"color:gold;\"></i>). Click the right arrow (<i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-right\"></i>) to start a new calculation.\n\nThe **Division format** buttons let you choose how the division is written: with the **Result below** the bar (as in many schoolbooks) or with the **Result on top** of it (a format that appears in historical mathematical documents).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4742a2d6-a3c5-4883-beb5-8ed0240fa9a7",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Long Division",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
